cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "dispatch-fidelity: dispatch-fidelity auditing for agentic systems"
message: "If you use this software, please cite the method deposit below."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Varga
    given-names: Zoltan
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0003-1020-834X"
repository-code: "https://github.com/glasseymour/dispatch-fidelity"
license: Apache-2.0
version: 0.3.0
# The version DOI, matching the `version:` field above. The concept DOI resolves to the
# latest release, so putting it here would make this file describe a different artifact
# every time a version ships — with `version: 0.3.0` still written beside it. Finding #27.
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.21841083
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.21841082
    description: Concept DOI for the software; always resolves to the latest version
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.21841083
    description: Version DOI for v0.3.0
abstract: >-
  A tool for measuring dispatch fidelity in agentic systems: whether a claim that a tool
  was called corresponds to an execution that actually happened. Ground truth comes from
  an out-of-band logging proxy with a per-run canary nonce committed before execution;
  scoring is deterministic; a run-binding check proves the manifest and the tool log come
  from the same run. Ships a fault-injection matrix so the instrument can be validated
  before it is trusted.
keywords:
  - LLM agents
  - multi-agent orchestration
  - tool use
  - audit
  - fault injection
  - reproducibility
references:
  - type: article
    title: >-
      Who Validates the Validator? Instrument failure in the shape of the hypothesis
    authors:
      - family-names: Varga
        given-names: Zoltan
    doi: 10.5281/zenodo.21812041
    year: 2026
